    Section(
      title: '+ Assess this patient\'s microstomia and oral commissure contracture. What splinting and prevention strategies are indicated, and how do you grade scar contracture severity?',
      content:
          '**Microstomia Assessment**:\n- **Normal oral aperture**: 45-50 mm (intercommissural distance)\n- **Functional minimum**: 35-40 mm (required for eating utensils, oral hygiene, dental care)\n- **This patient**: 25 mm — severely restricted. Cannot insert a standard toothbrush. Cannot eat with a fork. Cannot undergo standard dental examination or intubation.\n- **Cause**: Circumoral burn scar contracture pulling the commissures medially. Z-plasty provided temporary improvement but contracture is recurring (expected — scars contract during the proliferative/maturation phase for 12-18 months).\n\n**Scar Contracture Grading (Modified Vancouver Scar Scale components)**:\n- **Vascularity**: 0 (normal) to 3 (purple) — this patient\'s facial scars are erythematous (score 2)\n- **Pliability**: 0 (normal) to 5 (contracture) — firm to banding (score 4)\n- **Height**: 0 (flat) to 3 (>5mm) — raised 2-4 mm (score 2)\n- **Pigmentation**: 0 (normal) to 3 (mixed) — hypopigmented grafts with hyperpigmented scar borders (score 2)\n\n**Microstomia Prevention and Management**:\n\n1. **Oral Commissure Splint (Microstomia Prevention Appliance — MPA)**:\n- Custom-fabricated horizontal mouth splint that provides sustained outward stretch to the commissures\n- Worn for **prolonged periods** — initially 2 hours on/1 hour off during the day, and throughout the night\n- Must be comfortable enough for adherence; adjust gradually\n- Types: Dynamic (spring-loaded — provides active stretch) vs static (maintains position)\n- **TheraBite or Dynasplint oral system**: Commercially available option for progressive stretch\n\n2. **Oral Stretching Exercises**:\n- Active mouth opening exercises (maximal opening, hold 10 seconds, relax, repeat 10x) performed 4-6 times daily\n- Tongue depressor stacking for progressive stretch\n- Oral motor exercises: exaggerated vowel sounds, lip stretching, cheek puffing\n- **Goal**: Maintain >35 mm aperture to preserve functional eating and oral hygiene\n\n3. **Silicone Inserts**:\n- Custom-molded silicone conformers placed inside the oral commissures to provide sustained pressure and stretch\n- Worn under the splint or independently\n- Help maintain surgical gains between reconstructive procedures\n\n4. **Scar Management for Perioral Area**:\n- Silicone gel sheeting cut to conform around lips (difficult area — adherence is challenging)\n- Scar massage: Deep friction massage perpendicular to scar bands, 5-10 minutes, 3-4 times daily\n- Intralesional corticosteroid injection (triamcinolone 10-40 mg/mL) for hypertrophic bands — performed by burn surgeon\n\n5. **Coordination with Reconstructive Surgery**:\n- Each surgical procedure (commissuroplasty, flap reconstruction) will temporarily improve aperture\n- Aggressive splinting and therapy must resume within days of surgery to maintain gains\n- Communicate rehabilitation goals to the surgical team: adequate aperture for nutrition, hygiene, and eventually dental care and employment',
