    Section(
      title: '+ The patient\'s right lower eyelid ectropion is worsening. Describe the assessment and management approach.',
      content:
          '**Eyelid Ectropion Assessment**:\n- **Definition**: Outward turning of the eyelid margin away from the globe, caused by scar contracture pulling the lid inferiorly\n- **Severity grading**: Mild (1-2 mm scleral show), moderate (2-4 mm), severe (>4 mm with corneal exposure)\n- **This patient**: 3 mm scleral show = moderate ectropion\n- **Complications if untreated**: Corneal exposure keratitis, corneal ulceration, vision loss, chronic tearing (paradoxically — exposed eye tears reflexively), chronic discomfort\n\n**Conservative Management (current — bridge to surgery)**:\n1. **Eyelid taping**: Steri-strips or surgical tape applied to support the lower lid in a closed or near-closed position. Particularly important at night.\n2. **Lubricating eye drops**: Artificial tears every 2-3 hours during the day. Lubricating ointment (preservative-free) at bedtime.\n3. **Moisture chamber**: Clear plastic eye shield at night to reduce corneal drying.\n4. **Scar massage and silicone**: Gentle vertical massage of the infraorbital scar to stretch the contracting tissue. Silicone gel applied to the scar band.\n5. **Ophthalmology referral**: Regular corneal examinations to monitor for keratitis. Urgent referral if patient reports eye pain, redness, decreased vision, or photophobia.\n\n**Surgical Management (planned)**:\n1. **Full-thickness skin graft reconstruction**: Release the scar contracture and place FTSG (typically from postauricular or supraclavicular donor site for color match) to reconstruct the lower eyelid.\n2. **Timing**: Should be prioritized in the surgical reconstruction plan given the risk of corneal damage. This is a **functional priority** over cosmetic reconstruction.\n3. **Post-surgical rehabilitation**: Resume eyelid taping, scar management, and splinting immediately post-operatively to prevent recurrence.\n\n**Key Teaching Point**: In facial burn rehabilitation, **eyelid protection is the highest functional priority** — corneal damage can cause permanent vision loss. Eyelid reconstruction should take precedence over other facial reconstructive procedures if resources or timing are limited.',
