    Section(
      title: '+ Outline a holistic burn rehabilitation plan for this patient, integrating functional restoration, scar management, and psychological treatment.',
      content:
          '**Comprehensive Burn Rehabilitation Plan**:\n\n**1. Facial Scar Management**:\n- **Custom facial pressure garment (transparent face orthosis — TFO)**: Clear thermoplastic mask custom-molded to the patient\'s face providing 25 mmHg continuous pressure. Worn 23 hours/day. More effective than fabric face masks for facial contours.\n- **Silicone inserts**: Placed under the TFO at concave areas (nasolabial folds, perioral area) to enhance contact pressure.\n- **Scar massage**: 3-4 times daily, 5 minutes per region. Deep cross-friction technique. Use cocoa butter or silicone-based cream.\n- **Laser therapy referral**: Pulsed dye laser for erythematous hypertrophic scars — reduces vascularity, thickness, and itch. Fractional CO2 laser to improve scar pliability. Typically 3-6 sessions spaced 4-6 weeks apart. Coordinate with surgical plan.\n\n**2. Hand Rehabilitation (OT-directed)**:\n- **Web space splinting**: Bilateral C-bar or thumb spica splints maintaining first web space in maximal abduction. Worn at night and during rest.\n- **Active ROM exercises**: Composite fist making, individual finger flexion/extension, thumb opposition exercises, 4-6 times daily.\n- **Progressive strengthening**: Theraputty graded resistance exercises, grip strengthening, pinch strengthening. Goal: restore functional grip for ADLs.\n- **Desensitization program**: Graded texture exposure for dorsal hand hypersensitivity — start with soft textures (cotton, silk), progress to rougher textures over 4-6 weeks.\n- **Functional retraining**: Practice buttons, zippers, utensil use, toothbrushing, writing.\n- **Adaptive equipment**: Built-up handles for utensils and toothbrush, button hook, jar opener until grip strength improves.\n\n**3. Neck Contracture Management**:\n- **Neck extension splint/conformer**: Custom cervical orthosis maintaining neck in extension. Worn at night and during rest periods.\n- **Active cervical ROM exercises**: Neck extension, rotation, lateral flexion stretches 4-6 times daily.\n- **No pillow at night**: Prevents neck flexion contracture.\n\n**4. Oral Function**:\n- **Microstomia splint**: As detailed in Domain B. Progressive wear schedule.\n- **SLP referral**: Oral motor exercises, articulation therapy if speech is affected by lip scarring.\n- **Dietary progression**: Work with dietitian to advance diet as oral aperture improves. High-calorie, high-protein soft diet for weight restoration (BMI 19.2 — underweight).\n\n**5. Pain Management**:\n- **Neuropathic pain**: Optimize gabapentin (consider increasing to 800 mg TID or switching to pregabalin for better tolerability). Add duloxetine 60 mg daily for dual benefit (pain + depression).\n- **Opioid taper**: Patient on oxycodone 40 mg daily equivalent — develop taper plan. Target 10% reduction every 1-2 weeks. Replace with non-opioid alternatives.\n- **Pruritus**: Cetirizine 10 mg BID (less sedating than diphenhydramine). Add topical menthol 1% cream or pramoxine lotion. Massage and moisturizing reduce itch.\n\n**6. Psychological Treatment — Critical Priority**:\n- **Immediate safety planning**: Passive suicidal ideation requires safety assessment. Establish safety plan (crisis contacts, coping strategies, lethal means restriction). If ideation escalates to active plan, psychiatric hospitalization.\n- **Trauma-focused therapy**: Prolonged Exposure (PE) therapy or Cognitive Processing Therapy (CPT) for PTSD — both have strong evidence in assault survivors.\n- **Pharmacotherapy**: Patient refuses medication. Explore her resistance with empathy. Validate that "pills will not fix my face" while explaining that medication can reduce nightmares, improve sleep, and give her the emotional bandwidth to engage in therapy and rehabilitation. Prazosin 1-2 mg QHS for nightmares specifically (if she agrees). SSRI for depression/PTSD.\n- **Graded social exposure**: Structured, therapist-guided program to progressively reintroduce social contact — start with one trusted friend, progress to small groups, then community outings.\n- **Body image therapy**: Mirror exposure therapy (graded), self-compassion exercises, cognitive restructuring of appearance-related beliefs.',
