    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the victim advocacy, legal support, vocational rehabilitation, and community reintegration resources available to this patient.',
      content:
          '**Victim Advocacy and Legal Support**:\n\n1. **Victim advocate coordinator**: Assign a dedicated victim advocate (available through the DA\'s office or community-based organizations) who can:\n- Navigate the criminal justice process (restraining orders, court appearances, victim impact statements)\n- Connect to victim compensation funds (Crime Victims Compensation — available in all 50 states, covers medical expenses, mental health treatment, lost wages, and rehabilitation costs not covered by insurance)\n- Coordinate with the prosecution team regarding the patient\'s medical status and ongoing treatment needs\n\n2. **Crime Victims Compensation**: State-funded program that can cover:\n- Medical and rehabilitation costs (copays, deductibles, uncovered services)\n- Mental health treatment\n- Lost wages\n- Prescription medications\n- Transportation to medical appointments\n- Application should be filed as soon as possible (time limits vary by state, typically 1-3 years from the crime)\n\n3. **Legal aid**: Pro bono legal services for civil protection orders, civil lawsuit for damages, and disability benefits applications.\n\n4. **Safe housing**: Confirm current living arrangement is secure. If the attacker makes bail or is released, reassess safety plan immediately. Coordinate with law enforcement.\n\n**Vocational Rehabilitation**:\n\n1. **State vocational rehabilitation (VR) services**: Referral to state VR agency. As a victim of violent crime with acquired disability, she is a priority candidate.\n2. **Career assessment**: Dental hygienist role requires close face-to-face patient contact and fine manual dexterity — may not be feasible in the near-term. Explore:\n- Dental hygiene education/teaching (less direct patient contact)\n- Healthcare administration or dental office management\n- Telehealth or remote work opportunities in healthcare\n3. **Retraining funding**: VR services can fund retraining, education, assistive technology, and job placement services.\n4. **SSDI/SSI application**: If unable to work for >12 months (likely given planned surgeries and recovery), apply for Social Security disability. Burn injury with documented functional limitations and psychological disability qualifies.\n\n**Community Reintegration Program**:\n\n1. **Phoenix Society for Burn Survivors**: National organization providing peer support, community programs, and the World Burn Congress — an annual conference where burn survivors connect.\n2. **Burn survivor peer mentor**: Match with a female burn survivor who has navigated similar challenges — particularly someone who experienced facial burns. Peer support is one of the strongest predictors of successful community reintegration.\n3. **Changing Faces (international) / AboutFace (North America)**: Organizations specifically supporting individuals with facial differences. Offer social skills workshops, community events, and advocacy.\n4. **Gradual community re-entry plan**:\n- Week 1-2: Brief outing with mother only (drive-through, park with few people)\n- Week 3-4: Brief interaction with a store clerk or librarian\n- Month 2: Attend a small burn support group meeting\n- Month 3: Visit a trusted friend\'s home\n- Month 4+: Public spaces with increasing social complexity\n5. **Social media and online community**: Many burn survivors find initial social reconnection through online communities before in-person contact. This can be a bridge.\n\n**Financial Safety Net**:\n- **Crime victims compensation** (immediate)\n- **SSDI application** (3-6 month process, retroactive once approved)\n- **State VR services** (ongoing)\n- **Hospital social work**: Charity care applications for ongoing surgical costs\n- **Community fundraising**: With patient\'s consent, a victim advocacy organization can help coordinate community support campaigns',
