    Section(
      title: '+ How would you address this patient\'s profound body image distress and fear of social interaction following a violent, disfiguring attack?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Sit with the pain. Do not rush to reassure.**\n[Pause. Maintain eye contact. Let her see that you are not looking away from her face.]\n\'I am sitting here looking at you right now, and I want you to know that I see you — not your scars. I see a 28-year-old woman who survived something that should never have happened to her. And I am not looking away.\'\n\n**2. Validate the experiences she described without minimizing**:\n\'The staring, the pointing, the woman pulling her child away — those things happened, and they are painful. I am not going to tell you that people will not notice your scars, because they will. Some people will react with fear or discomfort because they do not know how to respond to something unfamiliar. That is their limitation, not yours. But it still hurts, and you do not deserve it.\'\n\n**3. Acknowledge that she is not responsible for what happened to her**:\n\'I want to say something important. What happened to you was a crime. It was done to you. You did not cause this, and you do not deserve to be punished for it by hiding from the world. The person who did this has already taken enough from you. I do not want him to take your whole life.\'\n\n**4. Provide honest framing about appearance and reconstruction**:\n\'Your surgical team has a plan for nasal reconstruction, eyelid repair, and scar revision. Over the next one to two years, your appearance will continue to change. Scars soften and flatten. Color fades. Reconstructive surgery can make a significant difference. You will not look like you did before — I want to be honest about that — but you will not look like you do right now either. This is the worst it will be.\'\n\n**5. Introduce coping strategies for social encounters**:\n\'There is something I want to offer you. We work with a psychologist who specializes in helping people with visible differences develop confidence in social situations. She teaches specific strategies — how to respond when someone stares, how to put a curious child at ease, how to walk into a room and own it. These are skills, and they can be learned. Some of the most socially confident people I know are burn survivors who have learned these techniques.\'\n\n**6. Introduce the idea of peer connection**:\n\'I also know a woman — a burn survivor — who had facial burns similar to yours. She was exactly where you are two years ago. She told me once that the turning point for her was meeting another survivor who understood. Would you be willing to talk to her? It could be by phone or text first — you do not have to meet in person until you are ready.\'\n\n**7. Gently challenge the "monster" narrative**:\n\'You said you feel like people see you as a monster. I want to challenge that word. A monster is someone who throws acid on another person\'s face. A monster is not the person who survived it. You are not a monster. You are a survivor. And when you are ready, your story has the power to help other survivors too.\'\n\n**8. End with immediate next steps — keep it small**:\n\'For right now, I am not asking you to go to the grocery store or face a crowd. I am asking you to come back next week, let our hand therapist work with you on your grip so you can hold your own toothbrush again, and meet our psychologist for one conversation. That is it. Can we start there?\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Non-verbal presence (not looking away), validation without false reassurance, naming the crime and redirecting shame from victim to perpetrator, honest surgical prognosis, skills-based social confidence building, peer support, challenging internalized stigma, minimal actionable first steps to avoid overwhelming.',
